.\" crystal_phonons(1) -- 2D lattice-dynamics playground
.TH CRYSTAL_PHONONS 1 "October 2026" "crystal_phonons 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
crystal_phonons \- interactive 2D phonon simulator with dispersion, S(k,\(*w), baths and defects
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B crystal_phonons
.RB [ \-n
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR demo ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR seed ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-shot
.I file
.RB [ \-\-frames
.IR K ]]
.br
.B crystal_phonons \-\-selftest
.br
.B crystal_phonons
.RB [ \-h | \-V ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B crystal_phonons
integrates the classical equations of motion of an
.IR N \(mu N
square lattice of atoms joined by springs and draws the result live.
The left half of the window shows the lattice; the right half shows
the analytic phonon dispersion along \(*G\(enX\(enM\(en\(*G with the
.I measured
dynamic structure factor S(k,\(*w) underneath it, a Brillouin-zone map of
the energy in every normal mode, tabbed parameter sliders, a lower plot,
and live diagnostics.
.PP
The model includes nearest-neighbour longitudinal and transverse springs,
diagonal next-nearest-neighbour central springs, Fermi\(enPasta\(enUlam
\(*a and \(*b anharmonicity, a checkerboard two-atom basis, random mass
disorder, point defects (heavy and light atoms, vacancies), a driven
atom, Langevin heat baths, and four boundary conditions.
Waves can be launched by clicking on the dispersion plot or the
Brillouin-zone map, or by poking the lattice with the mouse.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-n " N"
Lattice side.
Must be a power of two between 16 and 512; default 64.
The
.B N
key cycles through 32, 64, 128 and 256 at run time.
.TP
.BI \-d " name\fR, " \-\-demo " name"
Start in one of the demos listed under
.BR DEMOS .
.TP
.BI \-s " seed"
Seed for the random number generator (thermal velocities, Langevin noise,
mass disorder); default 42.
.TP
.BI \-o " dir"
Directory for recorded videos and screenshots; default the current
directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-shot " file"
Run for
.I K
frames, save a BMP screenshot of the window to
.IR file ,
and exit.
Useful with
.B \-\-demo
for scripted images.
.TP
.BI \-\-frames " K"
Number of frames before
.BR \-\-shot ;
default 120.
.TP
.B \-\-selftest
Run the numerical validation suite without opening a window and exit
with status 0 if every check passes.
See
.BR VALIDATION .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH WINDOW LAYOUT
.TP
.B Lattice (left)
Atoms drawn as squares (sized by mass), as bonds coloured by
longitudinal strain, or as a smooth heat map of a scalar field.
Overlays show the heat-bath strips, the absorbing sponge, the driven
atom, and a crosshair that moves at the predicted group velocity of the
last wave packet.
.TP
.B Dispersion (top centre)
Analytic \(*w(k) for every branch along \(*G\(enX\(enM\(en\(*G.
The yellow marker is the selected k; the short yellow segment is the
tangent, whose slope is the group velocity.
A dashed line marks the driver frequency.
With
.B S
on, the measured S(k,\(*w) is drawn underneath on a logarithmic,
per-column-normalised colour scale.
.TP
.B Mode energy (top right)
Harmonic energy in each normal mode over the full Brillouin zone
(\(*G at the centre, k\dy\u up), log scale, from a 2D FFT of the
displacement and velocity fields every frame.
For a two-atom basis the reduced zone is outlined in green.
.TP
.B Sliders (middle)
Four tabs: Lattice, Excite, Thermal, View (see
.BR PARAMETERS ).
.TP
.B Lower plot
Cycled with
.B L
or a click: energy time series (E, KE, PE, and the fraction of E in the
modes k, 2k, 3k, 4k for the selected k); the column-averaged temperature
profile T(x); or the quantum heat capacity C\dv\u(T) on log\(enlog axes.
.TP
.B Diagnostics (bottom right)
Time, total energy and its relative drift (closed systems only),
KE/PE, effective temperature, quantum C\dv\u at the target kT, group
velocity of the selected branch, heat currents (wall baths) or maximum
displacement, S(k,\(*w) progress, atom count, frame rate.
.SH PARAMETERS
.SS Lattice tab
.TP
.B K_L, K_T
Nearest-neighbour longitudinal and transverse spring constants.
.TP
.B K_D
Diagonal (next-nearest-neighbour) central spring.
Couples the x and y polarisations; with
.B K_T
= 0 it gives a central-force lattice with a finite shear modulus.
.TP
.B \(*a, \(*b
FPU cubic and quartic terms on the stretch of nearest-neighbour bonds:
V(s) = \(12K\dL\u s\u2\d + \(*a s\u3\d/3 + \(*b s\u4\d/4.
A negative or large \(*a without \(*b makes the potential unbounded;
runaway motion is detected and reset.
.TP
.B m_B/m_A
Mass ratio of a checkerboard two-atom basis.
Away from 1 the dispersion shows four branches and an optical gap.
.TP
.B disorder
Each mass is multiplied by 1 + w\(*x, \(*x uniform in (\-1,1), frozen
per site.
.SS Excite tab
.TP
.B amp
Amplitude of excitations and pokes.
.TP
.B \(*s_packet
Gaussian width of wave packets, in lattice spacings.
.TP
.B \(*s_poke
Radius of mouse pokes.
.TP
.B \(*w_drive, F_drive
Frequency and force amplitude of the driven atom.
.SS Thermal tab
.TP
.B kT
Target temperature for
.B T
(one-shot Maxwell\(enBoltzmann) and for the global Langevin bath.
.TP
.B kT_hot, kT_cold
Temperatures of the hot and cold wall baths.
.TP
.B \(*g
Langevin friction coefficient.
.SS View tab
.TP
.B exaggerate
Factor applied to displacements when drawing.
.TP
.B colour gain
Multiplies the colour scale.
.TP
.B steps / frame
Verlet steps per displayed frame (simulation speed).
.SH KEYS
.TP 14
.B Space
Pause or resume.
.TP
.B .
Single step while paused.
.TP
.B 1 2 3 4
Excite branch 1\(en4 at the selected k with the current shape.
.TP
.B Enter
Excite the selected branch again.
.TP
.B Arrows
Move the selected k by one grid point.
.TP
.B E
Excitation shape: standing wave, travelling wave, wave packet.
.TP
.B A
Toggle additive excitations (add to the current state instead of
replacing it).
.TP
.B T
Draw Maxwell\(enBoltzmann velocities at kT.
.TP
.B H
Bath: off, global Langevin, hot/cold walls.
With periodic boundaries the cold strip sits at x = N/2 so the two
reservoirs are not adjacent across the seam.
.TP
.B B
Boundary: periodic, fixed, free, absorbing (fixed walls plus a graded
friction sponge).
.TP
.BR D ", " Shift+D
Colour field: |u|, u\dx\u, u\dy\u, |v|, kinetic energy, div u,
curl u, mass (log\d2\u).
.TP
.B G
Style: atoms, bonds, heat map.
.TP
.B C
Colour scale: auto (slow exponential tracking, so changes in amplitude
stay visible for a second or two), locked, fixed.
.TP
.BR S ", " Shift+S
Show or hide the measured S(k,\(*w); clear its accumulator.
.TP
.B L
Cycle the lower plot.
.TP
.BR Tab ", " Shift+Tab
Mouse tool: poke, heavy atom (\(mu4), light atom (\(mu\(14), vacancy,
driver, erase.
.TP
.B P
Driver polarisation, x or y.
.TP
.BR N ", " Shift+N
Lattice size up or down (clears motion and defects).
.TP
.BR R ", " Shift+R
Reset motion; reset everything to defaults.
.TP
.B V
Start or stop video capture.
.TP
.B I
Save a BMP screenshot.
.TP
.BR F1 ", " ?
Help overlay.
.TP
.B F2 \(en F10
Demos (see
.BR DEMOS ).
.TP
.BR Q ", " Esc
Quit.
.SH MOUSE
.TP 14
.B Lattice, left
Apply the current tool.
The poke tool gives a radial displacement pulse on click and pushes
atoms along the drag direction while dragging.
Defect tools paint while dragging.
.TP
.B Lattice, right
Poke, whatever the tool.
.TP
.B Dispersion, left
Snap to the nearest allowed k on the path and excite the branch whose
\(*w is closest to the click.
.TP
.B Dispersion, right
Set the driver frequency to the clicked \(*w (places a driver at the
centre if there is none).
.TP
.B Mode map, left / right
Select the clicked k and excite the selected branch / select only.
.TP
.B Sliders
Drag, or use the mouse wheel to nudge.
.TP
.B Lower plot
Click to cycle.
.SH DEMOS
.TP 12
.BR F2 " packet"
LA wave packet; crests move at the phase velocity, the envelope and the
crosshair at the group velocity.
.TP
.BR F3 " focusing"
Point pulse on a strongly anisotropic lattice (N = 256, absorbing):
crossed elliptical fronts and lattice caustics.
.TP
.BR F4 " fput"
Fermi\(enPasta\(enUlam\(enTsingou with \(*a = 1: energy flows from mode k
to 2k, 3k, .\|.\|. and back (lower plot).
.TP
.BR F5 " breathers"
\(*b-FPU zone-boundary mode; modulational instability produces
localized breathers.
.TP
.BR F6 " impurity"
A light atom with a local mode above \(*w\dmax\u keeps ringing after
everything else has been absorbed.
.TP
.BR F7 " disorder"
Driven atom in a strongly mass-disordered lattice; high-frequency waves
stay near the source.
.TP
.BR F8 " heat"
Harmonic crystal between hot and cold walls: a flat interior T(x)
(ballistic transport).
Raising \(*b produces a gradient (Fourier's law).
.TP
.BR F9 " diatomic"
m\dB\u/m\dA\u = 3 with the measured S(k,\(*w) shown; thermal noise
fills in all four branches and the gap.
.TP
.BR F10 " driven"
Driver inside the band radiates; set its frequency above
\(*w\dmax\u (right-click the dispersion plot) and the radiation stops.
.SH PHYSICS
Units: lattice spacing a = 1, reference mass m\dA\u = 1, \(*h = k\dB\u = 1
where quantum quantities appear.
.PP
A bond along x between sites i and j carries
.RS
U = \(12K\dL\u s\u2\d + \(*a s\u3\d/3 + \(*b s\u4\d/4 + \(12K\dT\u t\u2\d,
.RE
with s = u\dx,j\u \- u\dx,i\u and t = u\dy,j\u \- u\dy,i\u (x and y
swap roles for bonds along y).
A diagonal bond with unit vector e carries \(12K\dD\u (e\(pc\(*Du)\u2\d.
The harmonic dynamical matrix is real in the e\u\fIi\fPk\(pcr\d
convention with true site positions; it is 2\(mu2 (monatomic) or 4\(mu4
(two-atom basis) and is diagonalised by Jacobi rotations.
Frequencies, eigenvectors and group velocities used for excitations and
plots come from it.
.PP
Integration is velocity Verlet with \(*Dt = 0.04.
Baths use an exact Ornstein\(enUhlenbeck velocity update after each
Verlet step.
S(k,\(*w) is the Hann-windowed power spectrum of u(k,t) along the path,
sampled every 6 steps over 512 samples (\(*D\(*w \(~~ 0.051,
Nyquist \(~~ 13.1) and accumulated over windows with 75% overlap.
The quantum C\dv\u(T) sums the Einstein function over every normal mode
of the ideal crystal; classical molecular dynamics itself always obeys
equipartition (Dulong\(enPetit).
.SH VALIDATION
.B \-\-selftest
checks: the eigensolver against the closed-form 2\(mu2 result;
standing-wave period against 2\(*p/\(*w; frequency and branch purity of
oblique travelling waves with K\dD\u coupling and of optical modes of the
two-atom basis; F = \-\(gr PE at every site for all four boundary
conditions with \(*a, \(*b, K\dD\u, vacancies and defects; energy
conservation of the anharmonic system; packet speed against the group
velocity; Langevin temperature; the Dulong\(enPetit and T\u2\d limits of
C\dv\u; and the position of the S(k,\(*w) peaks.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PHONONS_FONT
Path of the proportional .ttf font (default: DejaVu Sans, or Arial on
macOS).
.TP
.B PHONONS_FONT_MONO
Path of the monospaced font (default: DejaVu Sans Mono, or Menlo on
macOS).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I phonons_YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS.mp4
Video written by
.B V
in the output directory (requires
.BR ffmpeg (1)
with libx264).
.TP
.I phonons_YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS.bmp
Screenshot written by
.BR I .
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on normal exit or when every self-test passes; 1 on initialisation
failure or a failed self-test; 2 on bad arguments.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B crystal_phonons \-n 128
Start with a 128\(mu128 lattice.
.TP
.B crystal_phonons \-\-demo diatomic
Watch the optical branches appear in the measured S(k,\(*w).
.TP
.B crystal_phonons \-\-demo focusing \-\-shot focus.bmp \-\-frames 240
Render one image of the focusing demo and exit.
.SH NOTES
The window uses a fixed 1600\(mu900 logical layout and scales to fit the
display.
Video frames are read back before the buffer swap; recording stops if the
window is resized.
The browser build
.RB ( "make wasm" )
has no video capture or screenshots.
